"""Independent lessons in the mathematical order of book sections 13.1–13.2."""
import sympy as s
lessons=[];models=[]
def T(v):return s.latex(s.sympify(v))
def D(*rows):return r'\[\begin{gathered}'+r'\\'.join(rows)+r'\end{gathered}\]'
def E(code,title,question,solution,kind,**data):
 uid='MPROJ-E-'+code;models.append(dict(id=uid,kind=kind,**data));return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=uid)
def L(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
 lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='fm_projectiles_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method='<ol>'+''.join('<li>'+p+'</li>' for p in method)+'</ol>',mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_projectiles_'
L('components','Components, velocity and flight events','Use one clock and two component equations, then check that the event happens in flight.',
 'Resolve a projectile into horizontal and vertical motion and find physically valid event times.',
 '<p>Review constant-acceleration equations, resolving a velocity and <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_factorisation">solving quadratic equations</a>. Distinguish displacement, velocity and speed. All angles in a calculation must use one consistent angular unit.</p>',
 r'''<h3>State the model and the axes</h3><p>During free flight, model the object as a particle with no air resistance and constant gravitational acceleration. Take rightwards and upwards as positive. Write \(g\gt 0\) for the magnitude of gravity. Numerical exercises here explicitly use \(g=10\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}\).</p><p>If the initial components are \(a,b\) and the initial position is \((x_0,y_0)\), constant acceleration gives</p>'''+D(r'x=x_0+at,\qquad y=y_0+bt-\frac12gt^2',r'v_x=a,\qquad v_y=b-gt',r'v=\sqrt{a^2+(b-gt)^2}')+r'''<p>For speed \(u\) and an angle \(\theta\) above the rightward horizontal, \(a=u\cos\theta\) and \(b=u\sin\theta\). A downward launch has a negative upward component. Acceleration remains downward after the highest point.</p><h3>The highest point is a vertical event</h3><p>Set \(v_y=0\), not the whole speed equal to zero. For \(b\gt 0\),</p>'''+D(r't_{\rm top}=\frac bg,\qquad y_{\rm top}=y_0+\frac{b^2}{2g}')+r'''<p>The minimum speed is \(|a|\) if the highest point belongs to the flight being considered. Returning to the launch height gives the nonzero time \(2b/g\). That formula does not give the landing time on ground below a raised platform: solve the actual ground-height equation.</p><h3>Direction and perpendicular directions</h3><p>When \(a\gt 0\), the signed direction gradient is \((b-gt)/a\). State whether the direction is above or below the horizontal. For a leftward velocity, the gradient alone does not specify the quadrant. For two non-vertical directions, perpendicularity means that the gradients multiply to \(-1\); after clearing the denominators the condition is</p>'''+D(r'a^2+(b-gt_1)(b-gt_2)=0')+r'''<p>Check vertical cases separately if a denominator is zero. The displayed component identity also handles a perpendicularity condition without dividing by a velocity.</p><h3>Algebraic roots need a physical interval</h3><p>A height can be crossed twice, on the way up and down. Keep every positive root that occurs before the first ground impact. A speed inferred from an observation is invalid if the object would already have landed. For a concave height quadratic, positive height at both ends of an interval ensures positive height throughout it.</p>''',
 ['Choose axes, initial height and a time origin.','Resolve the initial velocity and write both position and velocity components.','Translate the event into an equation: height, vertical velocity, speed or direction.','Solve for every candidate and check the first-impact interval.','Give exact components or speed, direction and SI units as requested.'],
 ['Gravity changes vertical velocity, not the horizontal component in this model.','At the apex the vertical component is zero; the speed need not be zero.','The usual double-ascent flight time applies only to a return to the launch height.','A negative vertical component indicates downward motion, not a negative speed.','An algebraic root describing an observation after landing is inadmissible.'],[
 E('C1','1 · Position, velocity and speed are different answers',r'<p>A particle starts at ground level with velocity components \(9\) rightwards and \(12\) upwards, in \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). Take \(g=10\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}\). Find its position and velocity after one second, and its speed.</p>',D(r'x=9t,\qquad y=12t-5t^2',r't=1:\quad(x,y)=(9,7)',r'(v_x,v_y)=(9,2),\qquad v=\sqrt{85}')+r'<p>Distances are in metres and velocity components in \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). The particle is still rising because its vertical component is positive. Its first return to ground is at \(t=\frac{12}{5}\) s.</p>','state',a=9,b=12,h=0,t=1,g=10,position=['9','7'],velocity=['9','2'],speed='sqrt(85)'),
 E('C2','2 · Landing below the launch level',r'<p>A particle is launched from a platform \(24\) m above ground. Its components are \(8\) rightwards and \(7\) upwards, in \(\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). With \(g=10\), find its landing time, horizontal travel and impact velocity.</p>',D(r'0=24+7t-5t^2',r'(5t+8)(t-3)=0',r't=3,\quad x=24,\quad v_y=7-30=-23',r'v_x=8,\qquad v=\sqrt{593}')+r'<p>The negative root is outside this flight. The impact velocity points right and down; the speed is positive. Doubling the ascent time would return to the platform height, not ground level.</p>','landing',a=8,b=7,h=24,g=10,time='3',distance='24',vy='-23',speed='sqrt(593)'),
 E('C3','3 · Two crossings and the least speed',r'<p>A particle has initial components \(20\) rightwards and \(15\) upwards, from ground level. Take \(g=10\). Find when it is \(10\) m high, the separation of those positions, and its least speed.</p>',D(r'10=15t-5t^2',r'(t-1)(t-2)=0,\qquad t=1,2',r'\Delta x=20(2-1)=20',r't_{\rm top}=\frac32,\qquad v_{\min}=20')+r'<p>The flight ends at \(t=3\), so both crossings are valid. The vertical components at them are \(5\) and \(-5\); their directions are equally inclined above and below the horizontal.</p>','crossings',a=20,b=15,h=0,g=10,level=10,times=['1','2'],separation='20',minimum='20'),
 E('C4','4 · Infer components, then check the observations',r'<p>A particle starts \(20\) m above ground, moving rightwards. Its speeds at \(t=1\) s and \(t=3\) s are \(\sqrt{65}\) and \(\sqrt{305}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). Take \(g=10\). Find its initial components and check that both observations can occur before landing.</p>',D(r'a^2+(b-10)^2=65',r'a^2+(b-30)^2=305',r'800-40b=240,\qquad b=14',r'a^2=49,\quad a=7',r'y(3)=20+42-45=17')+r'<p>Choose \(a=7\) because the motion is rightwards. The first landing time is \(\frac{7+\sqrt{149}}5\gt 3\) s. The positive initial and final heights, together with concavity, confirm that no earlier ground contact invalidates these observations.</p>','infer',a=7,b=14,h=20,g=10,times=[1,3],speeds2=[65,305],landing='(7+sqrt(149))/5')])
L('trajectories','Cartesian trajectories, targets and obstacles','Eliminate time, retain every launch angle and apply the precise contact rule.',
 'Use a trajectory equation to find launch settings and complete physically valid obstacle intervals.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'components">components and flight events</a>. Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_inequalities">quadratic inequalities</a> and <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_roots">the discriminant</a>.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Eliminate the same time from both components</h3><p>If \(a\ne0\), use \(t=(x-x_0)/a\) in the vertical equation:</p>'''+D(r'y=y_0+\frac ba(x-x_0)-\frac g{2a^2}(x-x_0)^2')+r'''<p>For a rightward launch at an acute angle, set \(z=\tan\theta\gt 0\). Since \(a^2=u^2/(1+z^2)\), the trajectory becomes</p>'''+D(r'y=y_0+(x-x_0)z',r'\quad-\frac{g(x-x_0)^2}{2u^2}(1+z^2)')+r'''<p>The horizontal coordinate must lie in the actual free-flight segment. A parabola drawn beyond impact is an algebraic continuation, not the subsequent motion.</p><h3>A target can give two launch angles</h3><p>For fixed speed and a specified point, substitution produces a quadratic in \(z\). Find all real positive roots, then check the required flight interval. To compare arrival times, use \(t=(x-x_0)\sqrt{1+z^2}/u\). A larger angle can take longer and can be descending where the other trajectory is rising.</p>'''+D(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=z-\frac{g(x-x_0)}{u^2}(1+z^2)')+r'''<p>For rightward motion the sign of this gradient identifies rising or falling. Alternatively evaluate the vertical velocity at the arrival time.</p><h3>A wall is a set of allowed contact points</h3><p>A vertical face of height \(h\) requires its horizontal coordinate and \(0\lt y\le h\) when top contact counts but ground contact does not. Combine this with any condition such as being later than a given point. Different wording changes the endpoints. For a finite inclined barrier, solve the line intersection and then check that its coordinate lies on the segment.</p><h3>Clearance and optimisation are separate steps</h3><p>First find the full angle interval that clears a wall; only then minimise or maximise the requested range over it. Compare endpoints and stationary values. If top contact is excluded, an endpoint value may be an unattained infimum. The formula \(R=u^2\sin2\theta/g\) assumes a return to the launch level.</p><h3>Test reachability at the specified target</h3><p>A negative discriminant for the target's angle equation means that point cannot be reached at the fixed speed. This conclusion needs no general envelope formula. If a discriminant is non-negative, positivity of \(z\) and the physical flight conditions still need checking.</p>''',
 ['Derive the trajectory from the component equations with the actual initial position.','Use the target, wall or given trajectory to obtain equations in the unknown settings.','Find every admissible root or interval, preserving strict and inclusive endpoints.','Check direction, arrival time and contact with any finite segment.','For optimisation, use only the feasible settings and justify the endpoint or stationary choice.'],
 ['A trajectory starting above ground needs its initial-height term.','A fixed-speed target equation can have two valid positive angle roots.','The sign of the trajectory gradient needs the direction of horizontal travel.','The wall-top and ground-contact boundaries can have different inclusions.','A minimum over a feasible interval is not found by optimising over all angles.'],[
 E('T1','1 · Keep the launch-height term',r'<p>A projectile starts at \((0,2)\) m with components \((10,15)\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). With \(g=10\), obtain its Cartesian trajectory and highest point.</p>',D(r't=\frac x{10},\qquad y=2+15t-5t^2',r'y=2+\frac32x-\frac{x^2}{20}',r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac32-\frac x{10}=0',r'(x_{\rm top},y_{\rm top})=\left(15,\frac{53}{4}\right)')+r'<p>The same point is obtained at \(t=15/10\). The constant 2 records the height of the launch point.</p>','trajectory',a=10,b=15,h=2,g=10,polynomial='2+3*x/2-x**2/20',apex=['15','53/4']),
 E('T2','2 · Both angles and both arrival times',r'<p>A particle is projected from the origin with speed \(20\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). It must pass through \((20,10)\) m. With \(g=10\), find both acute launch angles and compare the arrival times and directions.</p>',D(r'10=20z-5(1+z^2),\qquad z=\tan\theta',r'z^2-4z+3=0,\qquad z=1,3',r't=\sqrt{1+z^2}',r't=\sqrt2,\sqrt{10}',r'\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{z=1}=0,\qquad\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{z=3}=-2')+r'<p>The lower angle arrives first, at its highest point. The higher-angle shot is falling. Both reach a positive target height before their first ground return.</p>','target',speed2=400,g=10,X=20,Y=10,roots=['1','3'],times=['sqrt(2)','sqrt(10)'],gradients=['0','-2']),
 E('T3','3 · A complete wall-position interval',r'<p>A projectile follows \(y=2x-\frac{x^2}{10}\), from \((0,0)\) towards increasing \(x\). After passing \((10,10)\), it strikes a vertical wall of height 6 m. Top contact counts; ground contact does not. Find all wall coordinates \(D\).</p>',D(r'0\lt 2D-\frac{D^2}{10}\le6,\qquad D\gt 10',r'y=6:\quad D=10\pm2\sqrt{10}',r'y=0:\quad D=0,20',r'10+2\sqrt{10}\le D\lt 20')+r'<p>On the descending branch after \(x=10\), the height decreases. The smaller height-6 root is on the earlier rising branch and does not satisfy the stated order of events.</p>','wall_interval',polynomial='2*x-x**2/10',height=6,after=10,lower='10+2*sqrt(10)',upper='20'),
 E('T4','4 · A target that cannot be reached',r'<p>A launcher has fixed speed \(5\sqrt{10}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). With \(g=10\), decide whether any acute angle can reach \((20,15)\) m from the origin.</p>',D(r'15=20z-8(1+z^2)',r'8z^2-20z+23=0',r'\Delta=(-20)^2-4(8)(23)=-336\lt 0')+r'<p>There is no real launch-angle tangent. Thus the target is inaccessible at this speed. This is a calculation for this point only, without an envelope formula.</p>','unreachable',speed2=250,g=10,X=20,Y=15,polynomial='8*z**2-20*z+23',discriminant=-336)])
L('stages','Meetings, rebounds and release into free flight','Use a common event, a separate clock for each stage and the correct transition conditions.',
 'Connect projectile segments without losing timing, velocity signs or the physical contact boundaries.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'components">components</a> and <a href="'+base+'trajectories">trajectories</a>. The rebound problems also use the coefficient of restitution. The string-cut problem additionally uses energy and radial force in circular motion; these prerequisites are summarised below and belong to later Mechanics chapters.</p>',
 r'''<h3>One meeting instant can mean two elapsed times</h3><p>Write a position equation for each object at the same physical instant. If the second launch occurs \(d\) seconds later and its elapsed time is \(s\), the first object has flown for \(s+d\). Set both coordinates equal and check that both objects are still in flight.</p><p>Equal gravitational accelerations cancel in a relative-position equation only when the time origins are handled correctly. A powered target maintaining height is not in free fall: use its stated motion rather than adding a gravitational drop.</p><h3>Start a new clock after an impact</h3><p>Position is continuous at the collision. The post-impact velocity becomes the initial velocity for a new projectile segment. For a fixed smooth plane, the tangential component stays unchanged while the signed normal component reverses and is multiplied by the coefficient \(e\), with \(0\le e\le1\):</p>'''+D(r'v_{\parallel}^{+}=v_{\parallel}^{-},\qquad v_{\perp}^{+}=-e v_{\perp}^{-}')+r'''<p>A vertical wall changes horizontal velocity only; a horizontal platform changes vertical velocity only. For an inclined wall at angle \(\phi\), resolve along the wall and a chosen perpendicular, apply the two rules and resolve back:</p>'''+D(r'p=v_x\cos\phi+v_y\sin\phi',r'n=-v_x\sin\phi+v_y\cos\phi',r'v_x^+=p\cos\phi+en\sin\phi',r'v_y^+=p\sin\phi-en\cos\phi')+r'''<p>Keep signed components through this calculation. Restitution scales the normal component, not the entire speed. For a finite barrier, a later intersection with its infinite line is another impact only if it lies on the actual segment.</p><h3>Release from a circle starts a projectile, not another circular arc</h3><p>Immediately after a deliberate cut, velocity is tangent to the circle and is continuous. The launch position is the cut point. Gravity then supplies the free-flight acceleration; tension is zero after the cut. For radius vector \((X,Y)\) of length \(a\), a left-and-up unit tangent on the upper-right arc is \((-Y/a,X/a)\).</p><h3>Check the pre-release circular motion separately</h3><p>For a particle attached by a light string, measured relative to the circle centre, conservation of energy relates the speed \(w\) at the bottom to the speed \(v\) at height \(y\). The radial equation gives the tension \(\mathcal T\):</p>'''+D(r'v^2=w^2-2g(y+a)',r'\mathcal T=\frac{mv^2}{a}-\frac{mgy}{a}',r'\mathcal T=\frac{mw^2}{a}-2mg-\frac{3mgy}{a}')+r'''<p>Along the rising arc this tension decreases with height, so its value just before the cut is the minimum over that arc. A string cannot supply compression: establish non-negative tension throughout. A deliberate cut does not require zero tension beforehand.</p><h3>Finish with physical consistency</h3><p>Check every meeting or contact occurs before first landing. Distinguish horizontal distance travelled from signed displacement after a leftward rebound. A second root belonging to a different stage or to a line extension is not automatically a physical event.</p>''',
 ['Draw or describe the objects, stages, axes and common event.','Give each flight its own elapsed time and write both component equations.','At a collision or release, retain position and determine the new signed velocity.','Solve the next segment and check all ground, segment and string constraints.','State distance or displacement as requested, with exact values and units.'],
 ['Delayed launches have different elapsed flight times at the meeting.','A powered target holding height does not acquire a free-fall vertical displacement.','Restitution applies to the normal component, not the whole velocity.','The cut-point height is not automatically the ground or the circle centre.','A line-extension crossing or negative tension can invalidate a proposed event.'],[
 E('S1','1 · A later launch uses a shorter clock',r'<p>From a point 40 m above ground, \(P\) is launched with components \((12,18)\). One second later \(Q\) is launched from the same point. They meet two seconds after \(Q\) starts. With \(g=10\), find the initial components of \(Q\) and the meeting height.</p>',D(r'P:\quad t=3,\quad(x,y)=(36,9)',r'Q:\quad 2a=36,\qquad2b-5(2)^2=9',r'a=18,\qquad b=\frac{29}{2}',r'h_{\rm meet}=40+9=49')+r'<p>The two particles have flown for 3 s and 2 s. Both begin above ground and meet above ground; concavity of their height functions rules out an earlier landing.</p>','delayed',a=12,b=18,h=40,g=10,delay=1,elapsed=2,Qa='18',Qb='29/2',point=['36','49']),
 E('S2','2 · A powered target does not fall',r'<p>A target stays 18 m high and moves left at \(4\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\), starting 44 m to the right of the origin. A ball is launched from the origin at the same instant, with \(\tan\theta=\frac{19}{18}\). With \(g=10\), find its initial speed and interception time.</p>',D(r'x_{\rm target}=44-4t,\qquad y_{\rm target}=18',r'x_{\rm ball}=at,\qquad y_{\rm ball}=\frac{19}{18}at-5t^2',r'18=\frac{19}{18}(44-4t)-5t^2',r'(t-2)(45t+128)=0',r't=2,\quad a=18,\quad b=19,\quad u=\sqrt{685}')+r'<p>Discard the negative time. At interception the ball has vertical component \(-1\), so it is just descending; the target still has zero vertical velocity because its height is actively maintained.</p>','powered',g=10,targetX=44,targetY=18,targetV=-4,slope='19/18',time='2',a='18',b='19',speed='sqrt(685)'),
 E('S3','3 · Resolve the rebound, then restart the flight',r'<p>A ball reaches a smooth vertical wall at height 12 m with velocity \((10,-2)\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). The restitution coefficient is \(3/5\). With \(g=10\), find its post-impact velocity and horizontal distance travelled before ground contact.</p>',D(r'v_x^+=-\frac35(10)=-6,\qquad v_y^+=-2',r'y=12-2s-5s^2',r's=\frac{\sqrt{61}-1}{5}',r'\text{horizontal distance}=\frac{6(\sqrt{61}-1)}5')+r'<p>Here \(s\) is time since impact. The leftward displacement has the negative of this distance. As the ball moves away from a vertical wall with constant negative horizontal component, it cannot hit the same wall again during this segment.</p>','rebound',a=10,b=-2,h=12,e='3/5',g=10,after=['-6','-2'],time='(sqrt(61)-1)/5',distance='6*(sqrt(61)-1)/5'),
 E('S4','4 · A cut preserves tangential velocity',r'<p>A particle moves up the right-hand side of a circle of radius \(a\), on a string fixed at its centre. The string is cut at \((\frac{3a}{5},\frac{4a}{5})\), with left-and-up tangent speed \(V\). Its subsequent flight passes through \((0,a)\). Find \(V^2\) and check whether the preceding arc from the bottom can have a taut string.</p>',D(r'(v_x,v_y)=\left(-\frac45V,\frac35V\right)',r't=\frac{3a}{4V},\qquad\frac a5=\frac35Vt-\frac12gt^2',r'V^2=\frac98ag',r'w^2=V^2+\frac{18}{5}ag=\frac{189}{40}ag',r'\mathcal T_B=\frac{229}{40}mg,\qquad\mathcal T_D=\frac{13}{40}mg\gt 0')+r'<p>Energy uses the rise \(\frac{9a}{5}\) from the bottom to the cut. Tension decreases along that rising arc, so its positive value just before the cut proves the string can stay taut throughout. Immediately afterwards the motion is a projectile; the old circular force equation no longer applies.</p>','cut',X='3/5',Y='4/5',targetY=1,speed2='9/8',bottomSpeed2='189/40',bottomTension='229/40',cutTension='13/40')])
